window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.3","title":"Errors and Power","goal":"Simulate thousands of experiments to see the Type I error rate when nothing is going on and the power when a real 6-point effect exists, then check with a power calculator.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Plan the experiment","text":"A teaching method may raise scores by 6 points (SD 15). We compare two groups of 36 students with a t-test at alpha = 0.05. The effect size d is the gap measured in SDs: 6 / 15 = 0.4.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nn, sd, diff, alpha, reps = 36, 15, 6, 0.05, 10_000\n\nd = diff / sd\nprint(f'effect size d = {d:.2f}')","out":"effect size d = 0.40","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate when the null is true","text":"We run 10,000 pretend experiments where both groups come from the same population. The share of experiments that still reject H0 is the Type I error rate, and it should be close to alpha.","code":"def reject_rate(true_diff):\n    a = rng.normal(0, sd, size=(reps, n))\n    b = rng.normal(true_diff, sd, size=(reps, n))\n    p = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, axis=1).pvalue   # one t-test per row\n    return (p <= alpha).mean()\n\ntype1 = reject_rate(0)\nprint(f'Type I error rate (no real effect) = {type1:.3f}')","out":"Type I error rate (no real effect) = 0.049","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate when a real effect exists","text":"Now the second group truly scores 6 points higher. The share of experiments that reject H0 is the power. With only 36 per group the test misses the effect more often than it finds it.","code":"power_sim = reject_rate(diff)\n\nprint(f'simulated power = {power_sim:.3f}')\nprint(f'simulated Type II error (beta) = {1 - power_sim:.3f}')","out":"simulated power = 0.387\nsimulated Type II error (beta) = 0.613","figs":[]},{"title":"Check with statsmodels and plan the sample size","text":"TTestIndPower does the calculation exactly, without simulation. power() gives the power for n = 36 per group, and solve_power() finds the group size needed for 80% power.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\nanalysis = TTestIndPower()\npower_exact = analysis.power(effect_size=d, nobs1=n, alpha=alpha)\nn_needed = analysis.solve_power(effect_size=d, power=0.80, alpha=alpha)\n\nprint(f'exact power with {n} per group = {power_exact:.3f}')\nprint(f'n per group for 80% power = {n_needed:.2f}, so use {int(np.ceil(n_needed))}')","out":"exact power with 36 per group = 0.388\nn per group for 80% power = 99.08, so use 100","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"power.t.test(n = 36, delta = 6, sd = 15)                # power for 36 per group\npower.t.test(power = 0.80, delta = 6, sd = 15)           # n per group for 80% power\n\n# simulation (random, so close to but not exactly the Python value)\nset.seed(42)\nmean(replicate(10000, t.test(rnorm(36, 0, 15), rnorm(36, 0, 15))$p.value <= 0.05))\nmean(replicate(10000, t.test(rnorm(36, 0, 15), rnorm(36, 6, 15))$p.value <= 0.05))","expect":"power = 0.3875 for n = 36 per group; n = 99.08 per group for 80% power (use 100); simulated rates about 0.05 and about 0.39","note":"power.t.test is exact for a two-sample, two-sided t-test, and n is per group. The two simulated rates change a little with every seed and will not match Python's digit for digit."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Power Analysis > Means > Independent-Samples T Test (enter power 0.80 to solve for the sample size)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / n delta sd.\nBEGIN DATA\n36 6 15\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE df = 2 * n - 2.\nCOMPUTE nc = (delta / sd) * SQRT(n / 2).\nCOMPUTE tcrit = IDF.T(0.975, df).\nCOMPUTE power = 1 - NCDF.T(tcrit, df, nc) + NCDF.T(-tcrit, df, nc).\nLIST power.","note":"The Power Analysis menu is the easy way and can also solve for n. The syntax shows the same exact power by hand, using the noncentral t distribution. SPSS has no built-in simulation here."},"stata":{"code":"power twomeans 0 6, sd(15) n1(36) n2(36)      // power for 36 per group\npower twomeans 0 6, sd(15) power(0.80)         // sample size for 80% power","note":"power twomeans takes the two group means (0 and 6) and the common SD. Stata reports whole group sizes, so it rounds the 99.08 up to 100 per group."},"try":["Change n to 100 and predict how the simulated and exact power change, then run it.","Change alpha to 0.01 and predict whether the power goes up or down, then run it."]});
